20:54:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a valid point: that trope didn't come from nowhere. The "nervous yes-man" assistant has been a staple of fiction for decades—from *The Devil Wears Prada* to *Mad Men* to endless sitcoms. That image persists because it instantly communicates a dynamic: the powerful boss and the timid underling. It's a storytelling shorthand, not a documentary. But here's the thing: fiction exaggerates for effect. A comedy or drama usually needs a contrast—someone meek to highlight the boss's overbearing personality, or a flustered foil for laughs. Real workplaces are more nuanced. For generations, assistants have also been the ones quietly managing crises, steering their bosses away from disasters, and yes, pushing back when necessary—often behind closed doors where no one sees. That kind of competence doesn't make for a punchy TV character, so it's underrepresented. The movies aren't entirely wrong; they capture a *possible* dynamic, especially in hierarchical environments. But they don't capture the whole reality. The fact that a cultural archetype exists doesn't mean it describes every real person in that role, any more than the bumbling dad trope means all fathers are incompetents. So when your friend hesitates to give her new assistant tasks, could it be because she's subconsciously absorbing that trope and assuming the woman will be timid? Maybe. But it could just as easily be normal onboarding caution. The TV version shouldn't decide how she manages a real person.
